<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0; font-size: 2em;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Where Is Your Book? – Lesson # 4</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> Kinder (CEFR: A1.2)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Where Is It?</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Where Is Your Book?</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2">
                <b>Specific Objective:</b>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Writing: productive: Label drawings of basic objects.<br>interactive: Engage in interactive labeling activities.</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2">
                <b>Learning Outcome:</b>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Writing: Can draw and label classroom objects and their locations (e.g., drawing a book on a desk and writing 'book on the desk').<br>Can engage in interactive labeling activities by labeling peer-created drawings of objects (e.g., labels a peer's sketch of a car with the word 'car').<br>Can trace letters and short words related to locations to label objects (e.g., 'in' or 'on').</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 30px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 35%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 15%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b><br>Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher leads a "Where is...?" TPR activity using real classroom objects. The teacher says, "Where is the book?" and points to a book. Students point to the book. Then the teacher gives commands like "Put the pencil on the desk!" or "Put the bag under the chair!" Students follow by moving the objects. (Vocabulary: book, pencil, desk, chair, bag. Prepositions: on, under).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up a real book and says clearly, "This is a book." (Pronunciation: /b/ in 'book'). Then, the teacher places the book on the desk and says, "Where is the book? It is on the desk." The teacher writes the word 'book' and 'on the desk' on the board in large, clear letters, pointing to each word as it's said. The teacher emphasizes the sound /w/ in 'where'.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher uses simple questions to check understanding.<br>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is the pencil on the desk?" (Students nod 'yes' or shake head 'no').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Teacher puts a book under a chair) "Is the book *on* the chair or *under* the chair?" (Students point to 'under' or say 'under').</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b><br>Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Where Is It?".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Large flashcards or a big picture chart depicting various classroom objects (chair, desk, table, book, bag, box, pencil) placed in different locations with prepositions (in, on, under, next to, behind). Each picture clearly shows the object and its location (e.g., a book on a table, a pencil under a chair). Each picture has a simple question 'Where is the [object]?' and a short answer 'It is [preposition] the [furniture].' printed below in large font. (e.g., "Where is the book? It is on the table.").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Using location words: Respond with basic words to location-based questions, using prepositions (e.g., 'The book is on the desk.').</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b><br>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher shows a picture (e.g., a pencil in a box) and asks, "Where is the pencil?" Students point to the picture and say the preposition, "in."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher shows two pictures, one with a book on a desk and another with a book under a desk. The teacher says, "The book is on the desk." Students point to the correct picture.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students choral repeat the simple location phrases from the flashcards (e.g., "on the desk", "under the chair", "in the bag").</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b><br>Accuracy focus on Writing</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Prepositions of place (in, on, under), common classroom nouns (chair, desk, book, pencil, bag), WH-Questions for Location ('Where is the book?'). Phonemic awareness: Recognizing letter sounds (K, L, M, N, O) through tracing words like 'book', 'pencil', 'marker', 'notebook', 'key'.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b><br>
                <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 1 (Controlled Tracing & Labeling):</b> The teacher distributes a worksheet with simple outlines of classroom objects (e.g., a book, a pencil, a desk, a chair). Below each outline, there are dotted lines for students to trace the corresponding word (e.g., "book", "pencil", "desk", "chair"). Students practice tracing these words, focusing on letter formation. The teacher circulates, providing support and correcting grip/strokes. (Phonemic awareness: Teacher guides students to identify the starting sounds as they trace, e.g., "B-b-book" or "P-p-pencil").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 2 (Semi-controlled Preposition Tracing & Matching):</b> The teacher provides a second worksheet. This worksheet has three simple drawings of an object and a box (e.g., a ball *on* a box, a ball *in* a box, a ball *under* a box). Next to each drawing, there are two preposition words written, one correctly describing the picture and one incorrect (e.g., 'on' / 'in'; 'in' / 'under'). Students trace the correct preposition (e.g., 'on') to complete a short label for the picture.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 3 (Interactive Object Labeling):</b> Students work in pairs. Each student receives a small blank piece of paper. Student A draws one simple classroom object (e.g., a bag or a ruler) and then exchanges the drawing with Student B. Student B then writes the name of the object (e.g., 'bag') below Student A's drawing. They then return the drawing to check if the label is correct. The teacher models how to politely ask, "Is this a bag, please?" and respond, "Yes, it is." or "No, it's a ruler."</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b><br>Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Where Is It?".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task format:</b> Individual drawing and labeling activity followed by peer interaction.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Description: "My Labeled Classroom Scene"</b><br>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Each student receives a large sheet of paper and crayons/markers.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Part 1 (Drawing):</b> Students draw a simple classroom scene including at least two pieces of furniture (e.g., a desk and a chair, or a table and a shelf). Then, they draw two or three classroom objects (e.g., a book, a pencil, a bag) in different locations relative to the furniture (e.g., 'book on the desk', 'pencil under the chair', 'bag next to the table'). Teacher reminds them to use colors they know (yellow, red, blue, green).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Part 2 (Labeling):</b> Below each drawn object and its location, students write a simple label. For Kinder, this can involve tracing pre-written words like 'book', 'desk', 'on', 'under', 'in' which the teacher has prepared as small labels on slips of paper or on the board for students to copy. Students can also attempt to write the full phrase, "book on desk" or "pencil under chair" if they are able, or simply label "book", "desk", "on".</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Part 3 (Peer Interaction):</b> Students then display their labeled drawings. In pairs, one student points to an object in their drawing and asks their partner, "Where is the book?" The partner attempts to read the label or uses known vocabulary to answer (e.g., "on the desk"). They can also ask politely, "Show me the red book, please."</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b><br>Formative assessment strategy</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the "My Labeled Classroom Scene" activity and informal peer assessment during the interaction phase.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students present their "My Labeled Classroom Scene" drawing, pointing to the objects and attempting to read their labels to the teacher or a peer.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b><br>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity of Drawing:</b> Are the objects and furniture clearly identifiable?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of Target Vocabulary:</b> Do the labels correctly match the drawn objects (e.g., 'book', 'pencil', 'chair', 'desk', 'bag', 'box')?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Application of Prepositions:</b> Are 'in', 'on', 'under' used appropriately in the labels or when describing the location orally?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Attempt at Writing/Tracing:</b> Is there a genuine effort to trace or write the words, even if imperfectly?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Intelligible Pronunciation:</b> When reading their labels or responding to peers, are the key words like 'book' (/b/) and 'where' (/w/) pronounced understandably?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaboration (Peer Interaction):</b> Did students engage in the peer questioning/answering activity?</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students are asked to draw a happy face or a neutral face next to:<br>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can draw a book and trace/write 'book'."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can trace/write 'on' or 'in' for locations."</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection:</b><br>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new words can I draw and write today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was my favorite thing to draw and label in the classroom scene?"</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b><br>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this writing lesson? (e.g., Were the tracing activities effective for Kinder? Did the combination of drawing and labeling help reinforce vocabulary and prepositions?)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for future lessons? (e.g., Do students need more practice with specific letter formations for K, L, M, N, O? Are the short phrases 'on desk' too complex, and should I focus only on single word labels?)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">How can I ensure more consistent use of polite requests ('Show me the blue crayon, please.') during interactive tasks?</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This writing lesson on labeling objects and their locations will be consolidated in the next lesson (Lesson 5: Mediation & Integration) where students will use their drawings and labels to describe their classroom scene to others, reinforcing both receptive and productive skills in a project-based activity.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>